================================================================================================
§4  Can W_j (not Var_j) cancel E[H_j]?  Cauchy-Schwarz ceiling on X = -2Cov(G,Phi')
================================================================================================
  On the a=0 SU(2) slice X == 0 EXACTLY (Phi radial => Phi' == 0).  So W_j is pinned at
  W_geo = 0.2051 (theta=pi, beta-free) and CANNOT cancel anything there: the cancellation is
  done 100% by Var_j = beta^2 V0.

  Off the slice X != 0.  Universal ceiling: |X| <= 2 sqrt(VarG * VarPhi')  (Cauchy-Schwarz).
  Measured inputs (archive, SU(3) L=4/6 pool, beta=5.7 / 2.5):
    VarG ~ 2.0 ,  VarPhi' ~ 0.02  =>  2 sqrt(0.04) = 0.400   vs   W_geo ~ 0.32
    => W_j >= 0.32 - 0.40 = -0.08  < 0   (allowed, and 0.08 ~ the whole N/12=0.167 floor/2)
    measured W_full = W_geo + X ~ 0.54 > 0.32  =>  measured X ~ +0.22 (POSITIVE at that point)
  => W_j CAN be negative enough to eat E[H_j] on general slices (there Ddyn = E[H]-VarG ~ 0,
     equipartition), but on the exactly solvable slice it is Var_j, not W_j, that does the eating.
     Either way (★) is a real inequality with a real failure window -- not a tautology.

